---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="2.5 Organisational Culture (HL)">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/">Unit 2</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>2.5 Organisational Culture (HL)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">2.5 <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></span>
    <h1>Organisational Culture</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">The way we do things around here: what shapes it, Handy's four gods, and what happens when cultures clash.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">HL Activities</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Define organisational culture and describe what shapes it (NORMS)</li>
        <li>Analyse a business's culture using Handy's four gods</li>
        <li>Link culture to structure and leadership style</li>
        <li>Explain the triggers and effects of a culture clash, and how to reduce one</li>
        <li>Use Hofstede's dimensions to compare national cultures</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">What is organisational culture?</h2>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Organisational culture</div><div class="definition">The values, attitudes, beliefs and assumptions shared by people in a business. In short: "the way we do things around here".</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Culture clash</div><div class="definition">More than one culture competes for dominance in the same business.</div></div>
      </div>
      <p>You can see culture in dress code, office layout, how formally people talk, rituals and stories about the firm's history. A clear culture can bring teamwork, higher productivity and lower labour turnover. A weak or toxic one brings confusion and people leaving. Culture works like a Herzberg <strong>hygiene factor</strong>: a good one stops complaints, a bad one drives people out.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">NORMS: what shapes culture</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Shaper</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>N</strong></td><td>Nature of the business</td><td>A hospital behaves differently from a design studio</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>O</strong></td><td>Organisational structure</td><td>Tall or flat</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>R</strong></td><td>Rewards</td><td>Pay, praise, promotion</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>M</strong></td><td>Management style</td><td>How decisions get made</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>S</strong></td><td>Sanctions</td><td>What happens when someone breaks the norms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Handy: the four gods of management</h2>
      <p>Charles Handy, <em>Gods of Management</em> (1978). Four types of culture, each named after a Greek god. Each one has a picture that shows where power sits.</p>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>The detail under each god (where power sits, typical businesses, strengths and limits) was added to fill out the lesson notes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Zeus: power culture</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Myth:</strong> king of the gods. He overthrew his father and ruled Olympus alone; every god answered to him.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Image:</strong> a spider's web. One powerful person sits at the centre, with lines of influence running out to everyone.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>How it works:</strong> few rules, decisions made fast by the boss and a trusted inner circle. Judged on results. Loyalty to the leader matters.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Found in:</strong> small firms run by the founder, family businesses, trading and sales teams.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>+</strong> Fast decisions, clear direction. <strong>−</strong> Everything depends on one person: what happens when they leave? Fear and "yes" people. Breaks down as the firm grows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Apollo: role culture</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Myth:</strong> god of order, reason and harmony, with everything in its proper place.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Image:</strong> a Greek temple. The pillars are departments (finance, marketing, HR), joined at the top by senior management.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>How it works:</strong> power comes from your job title. Job descriptions, rules and procedures. Everything goes through the proper channels.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Found in:</strong> large, established firms, banks, insurers, the civil service.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>+</strong> Efficient and predictable when things are stable; job security. <strong>−</strong> Slow to change, rigid, frustrating for creative people. Struggles when markets shift fast.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Athena: task culture</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Myth:</strong> goddess of wisdom and strategy, born fully armed from Zeus's head. She helped heroes by mentoring and solving problems, not by commanding.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Image:</strong> a net. Teams form where the strands cross, do a project, then re-form.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>How it works:</strong> power comes from <strong>expertise</strong>, not position. Project teams, often a matrix structure. Judged on solving the problem.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Found in:</strong> consultancies, advertising agencies, tech and R&amp;D teams.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>+</strong> Creative, adaptable, motivating for experts. <strong>−</strong> Expensive (experts cost a lot), hard to control, teams compete for resources. Less suited to routine work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Dionysus: person culture</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Myth:</strong> god of wine, theatre and freedom, who resisted rules.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Image:</strong> a constellation of stars. Bright individuals, loosely grouped.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>How it works:</strong> the organisation exists to serve the individuals in it, not the other way round. Little management; professionals run their own work.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Found in:</strong> law and medical partnerships, university research groups, freelancer collectives.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>+</strong> Highly motivating for talented, independent people. <strong>−</strong> Hard to manage or coordinate, little loyalty to the firm, hard to grow.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The gods compared</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Zeus</th><th>Apollo</th><th>Athena</th><th>Dionysus</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Image</strong></td><td>Spider's web</td><td>Temple</td><td>Net</td><td>Constellation of stars</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Structure</strong></td><td>Few layers, authority at the centre</td><td>Tall, narrow spans, long chain of command</td><td>Flat, project teams, matrix</td><td>Very flat, close to no hierarchy</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Leadership</strong></td><td>Autocratic</td><td>Bureaucratic, rules-led</td><td>Democratic</td><td>Laissez-faire</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Communication</strong></td><td>Centralised, top-down</td><td>Formal, vertical</td><td>Horizontal, open</td><td>Free, informal, two-way</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Motivation</strong></td><td>Results-linked pay, fear of the boss</td><td>Job security, clear roles</td><td>Achievement, recognition, teamwork</td><td>Autonomy, self-actualisation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Using Handy in an answer</div>
        <p>Most businesses are a <strong>mix</strong>: a firm can be Apollo in finance and Athena in product design. No god is "best". The right culture depends on size, stage of growth, how fast the market changes and the people. A start-up is often Zeus; as it grows it usually needs more Apollo.</p>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Link back to <a href="/ibbm/unit2/2-2/">2.2 structure</a>, <a href="/ibbm/unit2/2-3/">2.3 leadership</a> and <a href="/ibbm/unit2/2-4-hl/">2.4 motivation</a>: leadership style shapes culture, and culture limits which leadership style will work.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">When cultures clash</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Triggers</th><th>Effects</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Growth (new layers of managers)<br />Merger or takeover<br />A new leader</td><td>Confusion<br />Unhappy staff<br />Labour turnover<br />Resistance to change</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">External growth (mergers and takeovers) is the highest-risk trigger: two complete cultures meet at once.</p>
      <h3>Ways to reduce a clash</h3>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>This list was added to fill a gap in the lesson notes.</p>
      </div>
      <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
        <li><strong>Check culture before a deal:</strong> look at how the other firm works, not just its accounts</li>
        <li><strong>Communicate a shared vision</strong> early and often, and explain why things are changing</li>
        <li><strong>Involve staff</strong> in deciding what to keep and what to change</li>
        <li><strong>Keep what works</strong> rather than imposing one side's way on everything</li>
        <li><strong>Adapt leadership style</strong> to the people being led</li>
        <li><strong>Train and mix teams</strong> so people from both sides work together</li>
        <li><strong>Give it time:</strong> culture changes slowly</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">A compromise that combines rules without agreeing the values behind them often fails (see the Saigon Swift case).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Hofstede: national culture</h2>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>Set as homework in class. This summary was added to fill the gap.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Every business sits inside a national culture, which shapes what staff expect. Geert Hofstede's six dimensions compare countries.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>The question</th><th>At work</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Power distance</strong></td><td>How far do people accept that power is shared unequally?</td><td>High: staff expect to be told; Zeus or Apollo fits. Low: staff expect a say</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Individualism v collectivism</strong></td><td>Do people look after themselves, or their group?</td><td>Individual bonuses v team rewards and loyalty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Masculinity v femininity</strong></td><td>Is success about winning, or about care and quality of life?</td><td>Competition and status v work-life balance</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Uncertainty avoidance</strong></td><td>How comfortable are people with risk and ambiguity?</td><td>High: clear rules and procedures (Apollo). Low: comfortable with change</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Long-term v short-term orientation</strong></td><td>Focus on the future, or on the past and present?</td><td>Patient investment v quick results</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Indulgence v restraint</strong></td><td>How freely do people enjoy life and have fun?</td><td>Relaxed perks and social events v strict norms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Hofstede describes national averages, not individuals, and the data is old. Use it to spot possible clashes when a business works across countries.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Culture is "the way we do things around here"; NORMS shape it</li>
        <li>Zeus (web, power), Apollo (temple, role), Athena (net, task), Dionysus (stars, person)</li>
        <li>Culture links to structure, leadership and motivation; most firms are a mix</li>
        <li>Clashes come from growth, takeovers and new leaders, and cause confusion, turnover and resistance</li>
        <li>Hofstede's six dimensions explain differences between national cultures</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- HL ACTIVITIES -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Skim and note</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Skim your textbook (Hoang, pages 181 to 184). In your own words: what is organisational culture, and what types are there? Then for each NORMS letter, write what it stands for and one example.</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">NORMS</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">Nature of the business, Organisational structure, Rewards, Management style, Sanctions.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Concept map</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Build a concept map of organisational culture, including NORMS and the four gods. Add at least one link to organisational structure and one to leadership style.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Place each option under the right god</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each row, match the four options to Zeus, Apollo, Athena and Dionysus.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li><strong>Image:</strong> net / spider's web / constellation of stars / temple <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Zeus: spider's web. Apollo: temple. Athena: net. Dionysus: constellation of stars.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Structure:</strong> very flat, near-hierarchyless / flat, project teams, matrix / tall, narrow spans, long chain of command / few layers, authority held at the centre <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Zeus: few layers, authority at the centre. Apollo: tall, narrow spans. Athena: flat, project teams. Dionysus: very flat.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Leadership style:</strong> laissez-faire / democratic / autocratic / bureaucratic, rules-led <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Zeus: autocratic. Apollo: bureaucratic. Athena: democratic. Dionysus: laissez-faire.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Communication:</strong> free, informal, two-way / horizontal, open / centralised, top-down / formal, vertical <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Zeus: centralised, top-down. Apollo: formal, vertical. Athena: horizontal, open. Dionysus: free and informal.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Motivation:</strong> achievement, recognition, teamwork / autonomy, self-actualisation / job security, clear roles / results-linked pay, fear of the boss <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Zeus: results-linked pay and fear. Apollo: job security and clear roles. Athena: achievement and recognition. Dionysus: autonomy and self-actualisation.</span></button></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Culture in our world</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Choose three international schools in Ho Chi Minh City, or three of your own classes (including this one). For each, use NORMS, name the closest god and say why, and give one strength of its culture. Start from strengths.</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Then discuss: is there a right culture for a school?</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: Saigon Swift</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Saigon Swift began in 2019 as a food-delivery app run by eight friends from a shared apartment in District 7. Decisions were made over lunch, any developer could change the code, and hours were flexible. By 2023 the company had grown to 400 employees across three cities, and the founders began hiring managers to keep up. In 2024 it was acquired by Meridian Logistics, a Singapore-based firm with a strict hierarchy, fixed 8:30 to 5:30 hours and a 12-page approval form for any new project. Meridian replaced the founding CEO, Linh, with Mr. Hartmann, a former army logistics officer who held weekly reviews and expected reports to pass through three layers of managers.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Linh's team had always posted ideas in an open chat channel, so when engineers kept messaging Mr. Hartmann directly, he read it as disrespect, while the engineers thought he was ignoring them because he never replied. Within six months three of the best developers had resigned and a staff survey put morale at 4 out of 10, with several people writing that "this no longer feels like our company". Managers from both sides agreed a compromise: engineers could keep flexible hours in project weeks, but only if they submitted approval forms. Neither side was happy and the forms were mostly ignored. When HR then introduced a new performance review system, a group of long-serving staff refused to attend the training, saying "the old way worked".</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">Task 1: For each factor, use evidence from the case to explain how it created, deepened or revealed the clash.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li><strong>Growth</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">At 400 staff with new layers of managers, the founders' informal way of working no longer scaled.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Acquisition by Meridian</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Fixed hours and approval forms landed on a flexible, autonomous workforce, so two cultures competed for dominance.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Change in leadership</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A democratic founder was replaced by an autocratic ex-army manager with three reporting layers, which removed the engineers' voice.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Miscommunication</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Open-channel habit v chain-of-command habit; each side read the other as rude.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Unhappy staff</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Lost autonomy led to resignations and morale of 4/10.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>The compromise</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Flexible hours plus forms combined the rules without agreeing the values, so the forms were ignored.</span></button></li>
          <li><strong>Resistance to change</strong> <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Long-serving staff defending the old way slowed integration.</span></button></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="exercise-q">Task 2: Which god best describes Saigon Swift before the takeover? Which describes Meridian and Mr. Hartmann? Give one piece of evidence each.</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Answer</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">Saigon Swift before: Athena or Dionysus (decisions over lunch, anyone could change the code). Meridian: Apollo (strict hierarchy, 12-page approval form). Mr. Hartmann: Apollo with Zeus tendencies (reports through three layers, weekly reviews).</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Check your understanding</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>State two features of a power culture. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Centralised decisions, an autocratic leader, results-focused, few rules.</span></button></li>
          <li>Would a power culture suit a tall or a flat structure? Justify. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Flat but centralised: few layers, with authority held by the leader.</span></button></li>
          <li>Which culture suits project teams or a matrix structure? Explain. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Task (Athena): power sits with expertise.</span></button></li>
          <li>What is the relationship between leadership style and culture? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Style shapes culture, and culture limits which style succeeds.</span></button></li>
          <li>Which culture is most likely to use laissez-faire leadership, and why? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Person (Dionysus): experts need autonomy, not direction.</span></button></li>
          <li>A fast-growing start-up replaces its founder with an experienced corporate manager. Identify the likely shift in culture and one risk. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Likely a shift toward a role or power culture. Risk: demotivation and turnover among staff used to autonomy.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 7: Hofstede (homework)</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Revise Hofstede's six dimensions for the test. For each, give one way it could cause a clash when a business from one country takes over a business in another.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

